    decision:
      "Scheduled routine. Fetched all remote branches and re-fetched origin/main (picked up at 1c588ec0, PR #946) before starting — no live sibling PR beyond the unrelated stale draft #202, and the diff-gate (git log since the last verified-clean sha, fa2c51ab/57c53a88) touched only process-log files, so a full tiered research sweep was skipped per the waste-reduction rule. Re-read the observability thread in /docs/architecture and AGENT_PROTOCOL, which as of the prior scaffold/resend/webhook wiring passes still explicitly named 'a Convex-outage SQLite-fallback trigger' as the one remaining unwired failure-path class in src/lib/db.ts — deliberately deferred five separate times (the original scaffold, error-tracking-message-length-cap, resend-email-capture-error-wiring, stripe-webhook-payment-write-capture-error, commerce-order-ledger-write-capture-error) with the same stated reasoning each time: touching all ~84-88 console.warn('...falling back to SQLite...', e) call sites in one pass was judged too large a regression-risk for this file specifically (the most heavily-tested module in the repo), without first extracting a shared fallback-wrapper helper.",
    rationale:
      "Recounted before starting: grep found 88 `console.warn(...falling back to SQLite...)` sites in db.ts (up from the 84 last counted, reflecting intervening feature growth — e.g. cron-run-health-visibility's two new fallback sites). Of those, 13 already had captureError wired individually across several prior sibling sessions (getLeads, getLeadBySlug, listOrders, listOrdersByLeadSlug, getOrderByStripeSessionId, getOrdersByStripeCustomerId, listOpenCheckoutSessions, and the six-function Commerce order-ledger read family) — the remaining 75 write-path and read-path functions (upsertLead, updateLeadStage, recordLeadOutreach, every deploy-handoff/client-inquiry/checkout-session/marketing-pack/commerce-catalog/commerce-order/client-voice-pack/cron-run write and list function, plus recordLeadPayment/recordSubscriptionRenewalPayment and the other *_CONVEX_PRIMARY-gated writes) were still silent beyond a raw console.warn.\n\nRe-examined, rather than accepted at face value, the two reasons this had been deferred five times running: (1) 'too large a diff' — no longer true once mechanized: wrote a small script that walks db.ts, finds each console.warn block containing 'falling back to SQLite', identifies its enclosing function by name, and inserts `void captureError(e, { component: \"db-<fnName>-convex-fallback\" })` immediately after — the exact one-line pattern every already-wired site already used, applied uniformly instead of by hand one function at a time. This is mechanically equivalent to shipping the same fix 75 more times individually, just done in one pass with one full verification gate instead of 75. (2) 'no shared choke point, would need a wrapper helper first' — true but not actually a blocker: db.ts's fallback catches are already structurally identical (try Convex, catch, check shouldFallbackToSqliteOnConvexFailure, console.warn, fall through to the SQLite path), so the mechanical insertion needed no new abstraction, just automation of the existing hand-written pattern.\n\nA third, previously-unstated concern from the docs (raised specifically for read paths, not writes): a sustained Convex outage would fire captureError on every single call with no dedup, versus a webhook's natural per-event boundary. Did not build new throttling infrastructure to address this (the docs' own reasoning for not building it preemptively — 'premature complexity... before anyone has actually turned this on' — still holds, and most receiving endpoints, Sentry-style tools included, already dedup server-side). Instead mitigated the two concrete costs directly: (a) normalized every capture call — the 12 pre-existing `await captureError(...)` sites and all 76 newly-added ones — to `void captureError(...)` (fire-and-forget), so a slow or unreachable tracking endpoint (up to DEFAULT_ERROR_TRACKING_TIMEOUT_MS=4s) can never add latency to the actual Convex-fallback request path, during a real outage or otherwise; this was previously inconsistent (13 different call sites awaited, adding up to 4s of stacked latency per request during an outage) and is now uniform. (b) accepted the volume risk as a bounded, self-limiting cost: the read functions newly covered here (getDeployHandoffBySlug, listClientInquiries, getMarketingPack, listCommerceCatalogs, getClientVoicePackByLeadSlug, listCronRuns, etc.) are lower-traffic than the three hottest reads (getLeads/getLeadBySlug/listOrders) already accepted as live in the original scaffold rollout — per-lead detail views and periodic digest/cron reads, not every-page-load hot paths — so this extends an already-accepted trade-off rather than introducing a new one at a materially higher volume tier.\n\nOne real correctness note, not a functional bug: a handful of the newly-covered write functions (confirmed directly for recordLeadPayment and recordSubscriptionRenewalPayment, both called from exactly one call site each with requireConvex: true — WEBHOOK_CONVEX_PRIMARY) have a console.warn/fallback branch that is structurally unreachable for their only real caller today, since shouldFallbackToSqliteOnConvexFailure returns false and the function throws ConvexPrimaryWriteError before ever reaching the console.warn line — the newly-added captureError call there is dead code today, same as the console.warn beside it always was. Left it in rather than special-casing an exclusion list: harmless (never executes, no behavior change), forward-compatible (a future second caller without requireConvex would make it live automatically), and it keeps the pattern uniform across all 88 sites instead of requiring a future editor to know which subset was deliberately skipped and why.\n\nFixed one duplicate found during the mechanical pass: getLeads already had a hand-wired `await captureError` a few lines after its console.warn (with an explanatory comment about this file's own one-at-a-time wiring history); the automated insertion added a second identical call immediately after console.warn before the existing one was noticed. Removed the duplicate, kept the original (now normalized to `void`), deleted its now-inaccurate 'we deliberately don't sweep every branch' comment since this iteration is exactly that sweep.\n\nsrc/lib/db.ts — 76 new `void captureError(e, { component: \"db-<fnName>-convex-fallback\" })` calls (one per previously-unwired fallback catch) plus normalized the 12 pre-existing `await captureError(...)` sites to `void`; removed one duplicate insertion + its stale explanatory comment at getLeads. src/app/docs/architecture/page.tsx — new paragraph in the Observability section closing out the previously-open 'Convex-outage SQLite-fallback trigger' item. src/lib/process/business-decisions.ts — this entry. src/lib/process/prompt_process.tsx — the accompanying ITERATIONS entry. No NEXT_STEPS change — all five gated items (stripe-price-objects, lead-ingestion, marketing-content-scheduling, commerce-payouts-stripe-connect, github-actions-runner-unavailable) remain operator/legal/infra-blocked, unchanged. Diff-gate anchor moves to 1c588ec0 (origin/main tip, PR #946 merge).\n\nVerified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run full suite (3925 passed, 230 files — every existing db.ts consumer test that mocks captureError or lets it no-op via unset ERROR_TRACKING_WEBHOOK_URL still passes unchanged, confirming the new calls are genuinely inert when the scaffold isn't configured) + a full clean rebuild (rm -rf .next && npm run build, clean, same 64-route split, no new warnings). Grepped afterward to confirm exactly 88/88 db.ts fallback sites now call captureError (void or otherwise) and zero duplicates remain. No dev-server smoke check needed — this iteration only adds best-effort diagnostic calls behind an already-shipped, currently-unconfigured opt-in env var; no behavior change when ERROR_TRACKING_WEBHOOK_URL is unset (the default, and this repo's current deploy state).",
