      "Scheduled routine. Restarted this session's branch from a fresh origin/main tip (67d22fc8, PR #947) since the pre-assigned branch had fallen behind by two merges; git fetch --prune across all remote families plus mcp__github__list_pull_requests found no live sibling claim beyond the pre-existing unrelated stale draft PR #202. Dispatched a research agent (independently re-verified against source before implementing) to find the next unclaimed empty-backlog item per AGENT_PROTOCOL's tiered priority order, since every NEXT_STEPS entry is operator/legal-gated. It found: GET /api/cron/checkout-recovery and GET /api/cron/outreach-followups (both shipped same-day, one iteration before cron-run-health-visibility's own dashboard/digest wiring) call recordCronRunOutcome(job, { success: true, ... }) unconditionally after their per-item send loop, regardless of how many sends in the batch failed — only the third cron route, daily-digest, correctly threads its own success/failure into that call.",
    rationale:
      "Verified directly: both routes catch each per-item send failure inside the loop (never letting it throw), aggregate a `failures` array, call captureError when failures.length > 0, then unconditionally record success: true — including the degenerate case where every single attempted send fails (e.g. a revoked RESEND_API_KEY, a Resend outage, or a marketing-suppression-list edge case hitting the whole batch). computeCronJobHealth/cronJobsNeedingAttention (src/lib/cron/cron-run-health.ts) — feeding the new Pipeline health 'Cron automation health' card and the daily digest's own 'Cron automation needing attention' section, the entire reason cron-run-health-visibility shipped — can only ever flag a job as failing (isFailing = !row.lastRunSuccess) when recordCronRunOutcome was called with success: false. A systemically broken automation (0 of N sends succeeding, every day, indefinitely) was therefore invisible in exactly the surface built specifically so an operator wouldn't have to notice this by hand — the HTTP response body already correctly reported failed/failures per-request, but nothing durable ever reflected it.\n\nConfirmed genuinely unclaimed, not a deliberate design choice: both routes' own route.test.ts files already had a test named 'aggregates a per-{session,lead} failure without aborting the batch, and captures it' that asserted the HTTP response shape and the captureError call, but never asserted what recordCronRunOutcome received in that same scenario — the gap was never actually exercised, let alone reasoned about. Grepped business-decisions.ts for prior mentions of partial-failure/masks/success-despite-failures relevant to these two routes: no hits. The cron-run-health-visibility entry that shipped the dashboard/digest wiring describes wiring 'both the success and failure/catch branch of all three cron routes' but never distinguishes a whole-route thrown exception (correctly wired) from a per-item aggregate failure inside a successful route execution (the gap here) — a fresh nuance in a same-day feature, not a previously-considered-and-rejected option.\n\nFixed by computing `runSucceeded = failures.length === 0` in both routes and passing that (plus a summarizing error string on failure, e.g. '2 of 2 recovery sends failed') into recordCronRunOutcome, instead of a hardcoded true — mirroring daily-digest's existing all-or-nothing precedent as the simplest, most conservative semantics (a batch with even one failure now shows as a failing automation needing attention; the HTTP response body's own success: true is untouched, since the route itself did complete without a fatal/thrown error). Updated both routes' existing 'aggregates a per-X failure' test to assert the new success: false + summarizing error, and added a new explicit all-fail test per route (every attempted send fails, response still 200, but recordCronRunOutcome sees success: false) — verified both new/updated tests actually catch the regression by temporarily reverting the call-site fix (hardcoding success: true again) and confirming 4 of the (now 23) cases in the two files failed with a clear diff, before restoring the real fix and reconfirming green. No cron-run-health.ts or Convex/SQLite schema change needed — this is purely a call-site fix in the two route files plus their colocated tests.\n\nsrc/app/api/cron/checkout-recovery/route.ts, src/app/api/cron/outreach-followups/route.ts — the two-line-becomes-nine-line success computation, each. src/app/api/cron/checkout-recovery/route.test.ts, src/app/api/cron/outreach-followups/route.test.ts — one existing test updated per file, one new test added per file (23 total cases across both files, up from 21). src/lib/process/business-decisions.ts — this entry. src/lib/process/prompt_process.tsx — the accompanying ITERATIONS entry. No NEXT_STEPS change — all five gated items remain operator/legal/infra-blocked, unchanged. Diff-gate anchor moves to 67d22fc8 (origin/main tip, PR #947 merge).\n\nVerified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run full suite (3927 passed, 230 files, +2 net new) + a full clean rebuild (rm -rf .next && npm run build, clean, same route split, no new warnings). No dev-server smoke check needed — pure server-route logic change fully exercised by the existing and new automated route-level tests; no new UI surface, and no live CRON_SECRET/Resend credentials in this environment to smoke-test the dormant routes end to end.",
